      "message": "But that\u0027s an interesting question: would any one else be interested about an APF packet filter being installed? Or (since can only have one - true?) then what if someone installed a filter, then another client installed another filter. How would the first client find out? This API was one way.",
      "parentUuid": "9f7dbc55_2e794c84",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 260,
        "startChar": 19,
        "endLine": 260,
        "endChar": 72
      },
      "revId": "0929b268a331c3a312d76f70a42c092028406427",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9f7dbc55_b0bdbace",
        "filename": "wifi/1.0/IWifiStaIface.hal",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 260,
      "author": {
        "id": 1074459
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-10-12T00:28:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "All of these operations (bgscan, apf) are designed to have only one instance running on a given iface.\nEven with the notification, this is potentially still a problem. Each request (by any client) will override the previous request. \nBy separating out the iface objects (NAN vs rest), we should be able to live with each interface being solely controlled by a single client. I don\u0027t see a use case for different clients to be interested in controlling the same iface object. \n\nBut, I guess this should be explicitly documented or enforced somehow. IWifi/IWifiChip objects can support multiple clients, but all the iface objects should be tied to a single client.",
      "parentUuid": "9f7dbc55_50dd4e62",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 260,
        "startChar": 19,
        "endLine": 260,
        "endChar": 72
      },
      "revId": "0929b268a331c3a312d76f70a42c092028406427",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
